    Thesis
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: Why AI Agents Require Native Semantic Grammar
        Purpose: Rationale for replacing human-oriented visual interfaces and syntax with explicit grammar-level semantics

        HumanVsAgent
            Type: Text
            Caption: Human Perception vs. Machine Semantics
            Content: Existing web interfaces (HTML/DOM/JS), document markups, and programming languages were designed around human visual perception. AI agents waste context capacity inferring structure from visual markup and punctuation, while remaining vulnerable to prompt injection across blurred data boundaries. The STF ecosystem encodes hierarchy, semantics, and trust provenance directly in the grammar.

    Components
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: The 5 Components of the STF Ecosystem
        Purpose: Detailed directory of all 5 specifications and tools in the STF stack

        ComponentSTF
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 1. STF — Semantic Text Format
            Content: The core of the ecosystem: an explicit semantic section tree, grammar-enforced trust zones (System, UserContent), native EBNF syntactic protection against prompt injection, and reduced inference cost for smaller models.
            Navigate: /ecosystem/stf/index.sil

        ComponentSIL
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 2. SIL — Semantic Interface Layer
            Content: Application extension of STF enabling AI agents to navigate and interact with websites without parsing raw HTML/DOM or executing JavaScript, organized into 5 conformance profiles.
            Navigate: /ecosystem/sil/index.sil

        ComponentSTFMemory
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 3. STF-Memory — Structured Long-Term Agent Memory
            Content: Structural state model for long-horizon agent memory featuring explicit conflict tracking, trust provenance, and views/projections instead of flat append-only logs.
            Navigate: /ecosystem/stf-memory/index.sil

        ComponentSIR
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 4. SIR — Semantic Intermediate Representation
            Content: Executable semantic intermediate representation built on STF for coding agents and verifiable compilation to WebAssembly.
            Navigate: /ecosystem/sir/index.sil

        ComponentSIRCompiler
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 5. SIR Compiler — Self-Hosting SIR-to-WASM Compiler
            Content: Self-compiling compiler written in SIR itself (~103 KB binary, proven byte-for-byte self-compilation fixpoint, 253/253 tests), demonstrating structural durability under aggressive context autocompression.
            Navigate: /ecosystem/sir-compiler/index.sil

